/// Chapter 18 of the guide, written from md-docs/18-tooling.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page Tooling(path: "/docs/guide/tooling", title: "Tooling", description: "Every wf command, the dev server, diagnostics, the formatter, tests, the gallery and registry, and editor support.", layout: DocsShell("Tooling", chapter: "Chapter 18", blurb: "One binary does everything: scaffold, build, serve, format, test, render and describe. A language server brings the checks into the editor.", sections: [Anchor(id: "the-commands", label: "The commands"), Anchor(id: "wf-verify", label: "wf verify"), Anchor(id: "wf-migrate", label: "wf migrate"), Anchor(id: "wf-serve", label: "wf serve"), Anchor(id: "wf-build", label: "wf build"), Anchor(id: "diagnostics", label: "Diagnostics"), Anchor(id: "wf-fmt", label: "wf fmt"), Anchor(id: "wf-test", label: "wf test"), Anchor(id: "wf-generate", label: "wf generate"), Anchor(id: "wf-docs-wf-registry-wf-types", label: "wf docs, wf registry, wf types"), Anchor(id: "editors", label: "Editors"), Anchor(id: "project-layout-as-a-reminder", label: "Project layout, as a reminder")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/outputs", prevLabel: "17. Outputs", nextTo: "/docs/guide/security", nextLabel: "19. Security")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("One binary, `wf`, does everything: scaffolds, builds, serves, formats, tests, renders and describes. A language server, `wf-lsp`, brings the same checks into the editor.")

        Heading("The commands", id: "the-commands").h2

        Table(caption: "The commands") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Command").header
                    Table.Cell("Does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf init NAME [-t spa|static|pdf|slides]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Creates a project from a template")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf build [-d DIR] [--stats]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Compiles `src/` to the output directory; fails on errors, prints warnings. `--stats` reports what it weighs") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf serve [-d DIR]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Builds, serves on `dev.port` (3000), rebuilds on every save and reloads the browser") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf fmt [PATH] [--check] [--stdout] [--to wf|wfx]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Formats source files; converts layouts")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf test [PATH] [--update]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Runs `test \"…\" \{ \}` declarations against their expectations and snapshots") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf generate page|component|store NAME`") }
                    Table.Cell("Adds a file with a starter declaration")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf render FILE --data JSON [-f html|html-fragment|pdf|slides] [-o OUT]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Renders a template with data ([chapter 17](/docs/guide/outputs#templates-rendering-with-data-on-a-server))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf migrate [PATH] [--check] [--stdout] [--wfx]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Rewrites WebFluent 2 sources to 3, then carries the project to 4")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf docs [-o DIR]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Writes a component gallery — every built-in and everything the project declares")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf registry [--json]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Describes every built-in: props, cases, flags, events, slots, parts")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf types [--json]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Describes what the project declares: enums, types, components, stores, pages, externals")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf audit [PATH] [--json]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("What the project trusts: markup, other origins, storage, `env`, the policy, the dependencies") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf verify [PATH] [--json] [--budget MS]`") }
                    Table.Cell("Loads every page in a real browser: errors, failed requests, paint timing")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("wf verify", id: "wf-verify").h2

        Markdown("A build says what the compiler wrote. This says what a browser does with it.")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build && wf verify")
            ])

        Markdown("It starts a headless Chrome, serves the output, and opens every route the project has. A route fails on an uncaught exception, a `console.error`, a file that did not arrive, an image that failed to load, or a page that rendered no text — the things a compiler cannot see and a reader always can.")

        CodeBlock(#"  16 route(s) in http://127.0.0.1:39785
    ok   /                                    236ms     613 nodes    447.0 kB   11 req
    ok   /app/deployments                      68ms     657 nodes    360.6 kB   10 req

  16 page(s), 0 problem(s)"#, title: "")

        Markdown("The numbers are the first contentful paint, the elements in the document once it settled, the bytes the page fetched and the requests it made. `--budget 400` fails a route that takes longer than that to paint. `--json` is the same report for a pipeline.\n\nIt also says **which built-ins no page drew**. A component can be in the registry, in the tests and in the documentation and still be broken in a page; the classes the pages carried are the list of what actually ran, so the difference is what nothing has exercised. One project is not expected to draw them all — it is a fact about coverage, not a failure.\n\nIt needs a Chrome or Chromium on the machine, or `WF_CHROME` pointing at one, and says so plainly when there is none.")

        Heading("wf migrate", id: "wf-migrate").h2

        Markdown("Two things, in one command.\n\n**2 → 3** rewrites every `.wf` under `src/` in place, with a note for anything that needed a decision. It is a change of spelling: the migrated project builds to what it built before.\n\n**3 → 4** is a change of what the compiler allows, so it runs over the project rather than the files. It adds every `env` name your pages already read to `public_env` — preserving what the project did, and printing the list, because *public* means anyone who opens the site may read the value. Then it names, with file and line, everything 4 refuses that 3 allowed: an `on*` attribute, a `javascript:` or `data:` URL. Those have no automatic rewrite; the old value was a string of JavaScript or a link that ran one. Finally it states the changes that need no edit: a store is built on first read rather than at boot, a `persist` value follows the site's other tabs, and `WF.store`/`WF.host` were renamed.\n\n`--check` writes nothing and tells you what it would do.")

        Heading("wf serve", id: "wf-serve").h2

        Markdown("The development loop. It builds once, serves the output, watches `src/`, `public/` and the config, rebuilds on a change and reloads every open tab. A build that fails leaves the last good page up and draws the error over it — file, line, message and hint — until the next save fixes it. The overlay is served at `/__wf/dev.js`, the build's state at `/__wf/status` (JSON), for anything else that wants to know.")

        Heading("wf build", id: "wf-build").h2

        Markdown("Runs the whole pipeline: parse → semantic checks → type checks → linters → codegen. Errors stop the build with the file, line, column, message and a hint; warnings print and the build goes on. The exit code is non-zero on an error, so a CI step is just `wf build`.")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build && rsync -a dist/ user@host:/var/www/site/")
            ])

        Markdown("`--stats` prints what the build weighs — every text file with its gzipped size, and which of the runtime's feature modules it carries. The runtime a page ships is the part of it the program reaches: `each` for a `for`, `router` for a second page, `net` for a `resource`, and, in the icon table, only the glyphs the program names.")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build --stats")
            ])

        CodeBlock(#"  What it weighs
    app.js                                15.8 kB  (5.3 kB gzipped)
    styles.css                            19.0 kB  (4.1 kB gzipped)
    total                                 36.3 kB

  Runtime: 4 of 28 modules, 24.9 kB of 107.2 kB (before minifying)
    kept     core pages store icons
    left out motion helpers format form text when match slot each show …"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("A name the build cannot see — an icon named by an API response, or a hand-written `<script>` that calls `WF` — is the one case this gets wrong; `\"build\": \{ \"runtime\": \"full\" \}` ships every module.\n\nEach build records what it weighed in `.wf-sizes.json` beside the project, so the next `--stats` shows what moved. `build.budget` names a gzipped size an output should stay under; going over prints a warning, never an error:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "build": { "budget": { "app.js": "40 kB", "styles.css": "30 kB" } } }"#, title: "json")

        Heading("Diagnostics", id: "diagnostics").h2

        Markdown("Every finding carries a code. The families:\n\nEvery finding carries its code, the file and the line, and a hint. The code is the thing to search for, so each one is listed below — none of them is a range you have to guess your way inside.\n\n**Errors stop a build. Warnings do not**, and nothing is a warning that the compiler could have decided for itself.")

        Heading("Types — T01–T12", id: "types-t01-t12").h3

        Markdown("Anything the checker cannot resolve is `Any`, which agrees with everything, so a program that declares no types raises none of these. Every annotation you add narrows what it can say. Fully covered in [chapter 10](/docs/guide/types#the-checks).")

        Table(caption: "Types — T01–T12") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it means").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T01`") }
                    Table.Cell("A value of the wrong type — given to a prop, a state, a field, a parameter or an assignment")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T02`") }
                    Table.Cell("A case the enum does not have")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T04`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A value that may be `null`, read as if it were not. `if let`, `??` or a `!= null` check narrows it") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T05`") }
                    Table.Cell("A field or method a record does not have")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T06`") }
                    Table.Cell("A member an object does not have: a store's, or a service's endpoint")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T07`") }
                    Table.Cell("A list, record or action used as a condition, which is always true")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T08`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `for` over something that is not a list") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T09`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `emit` whose arguments do not match the event it names") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T10`") }
                    Table.Cell("A call with the wrong number or kind of arguments")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T11`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `match` on something that is neither a resource nor an enum, or with arms of the wrong kind") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T12`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Secret` where it would escape — shown, spliced into text, logged, or kept with `persist`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Two more errors have no code because they cannot be anything else: an `on*` attribute (script written as an attribute) and an `href`, `src` or `to` literal naming a scheme a browser runs. Both are covered in [chapter 19](/docs/guide/security).")

        Heading("Accessibility — A01–A15", id: "accessibility-a01-a15").h3

        Markdown("Warnings. A control is named by `label:`, or by `aria-label:` / `aria-labelledby:` where its visible label is a separate element. The rules behind them are in [spec/ACCESSIBILITY_SPEC.md](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/blob/master/spec/ACCESSIBILITY_SPEC.md).")

        Table(caption: "Accessibility — A01–A15") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it means").header
                    Table.Cell("What to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `Image` with no `alt`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Image(src: \"…\", alt: \"What it shows\")`. A decorative image takes `alt: \"\"`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A02`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `IconButton` with no accessible name") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`IconButton(icon: \"close\", label: \"Close\")` — the label is never visible text") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A03`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `Input` with neither `label:` nor `placeholder:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Input(label: \"Username\").text`. A placeholder alone is a weaker answer, and disappears as you type") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A04`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Checkbox`, `Radio`, `Switch`, `Slider` or `Textarea` with no `label:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Checkbox(bind: agreed, label: \"I agree\")`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A05`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Button` with no text") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Give it text, or an `aria-label:` when its content is an icon") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A06`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Link` with no text") }
                    Table.Cell("Same. \"Read more\" ×12 on a page is legal and unhelpful; name the destination")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A07`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Heading` with no text") }
                    Table.Cell("An empty heading breaks the outline a screen reader navigates by")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A08`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Modal` or `Dialog` with no `title:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Modal(visible: open, title: \"Confirm\")` — it is the dialog's accessible name") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A09`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Video` with no `controls`, or a `Video`/`Audio` with no `captions:`/`transcript:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Video(src: \"…\", captions: \"/clip.en.vtt\").controls`. A video nobody can hear is a video nobody can follow") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A10`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Table` with no header row") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Wrap the first row in `Table.Head`, so each cell is a `<th scope=\"col\">`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A11`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A heading level skipped — `h2` straight to `h4`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The outline is how a page is navigated, not how big the text is; style with `style \{ font-size \}`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A12`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A page with no `h1`") }
                    Table.Cell("Every page needs one, and one is usually enough")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A13`") }
                    Table.Cell("A colour pairing in the theme below the WCAG AA contrast ratio")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Only checkable because a `theme` is parsed source. Adjust the token") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A14`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `role:` that requires particular children holding something else — `tablist` → `tab`, `list` → `listitem`, `menu` → `menuitem`") }
                    Table.Cell("Use the built-in that carries the role, or correct the children")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A15`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A control whose `aria-label` does not contain its visible text") }
                    Table.Cell("What a voice-control user says is what they see. Make the label start with the visible words")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("`A11` and `A12` do not apply to `Presentation` or `Document` output, where an `h1` per slide or per section is correct.")

        Heading("Search and sharing — S01–S04", id: "search-and-sharing-s01-s04").h3

        Markdown("Warnings. [Chapter 3](/docs/guide/pages-and-routing) covers what a page declares.")

        Table(caption: "Search and sharing — S01–S04") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it means").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`S01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A page with no `title:` — it has nothing to show as a search result's link, or in the tab") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`S02`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A page with no `description:`, so the search snippet is written for you out of the page's text") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`S03`") }
                    Table.Cell("A description over about 160 characters, which a search result truncates mid-sentence")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`S04`") }
                    Table.Cell("Two pages claiming the same route. Only one of them will ever render")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("What is kept in the browser — P01–P03", id: "what-is-kept-in-the-browser-p01-p03").h3

        Markdown("Warnings. [Chapter 9](/docs/guide/stores#keeping-a-value-across-visits) covers the policy.")

        Table(caption: "What is kept in the browser — P01–P03") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it means").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`P01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `persist` at `version: n` with no `migrate` step bringing some older version forward. A reader who last visited then loses what they had") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`P02`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `migrate` step above the declared `version:`, or with no `version:` at all — it never runs") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`P03`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `persist` inside a `store(scope: .route)`. The route change drops the store and the next read builds it again from storage, so the value comes straight back") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Declared and never read — U01–U05", id: "declared-and-never-read-u01-u05").h3

        Markdown("Warnings. A name beginning `_` is understood to be unused on purpose and raises none of these.")

        Table(caption: "Declared and never read — U01–U05") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it means").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`U01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `state` nothing reads. Assigning to it is not reading it") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`U02`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `derived` nothing reads") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`U03`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A component nothing places, names as a `layout:`, or reaches as a part") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`U04`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A store member — state, derived or action — nothing reads, inside the store or as `Store.member`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`U05`") }
                    Table.Cell("An action nothing calls")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Vocabulary — V01–V03", id: "vocabulary-v01-v03").h3

        Markdown("Warnings.")

        Table(caption: "Vocabulary — V01–V03") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it means").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A bare word in argument position that nothing in scope declares — a misspelled name, or a flag written without its dot (`center` where `.center` was meant)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V02`") }
                    Table.Cell("A flag whose class no stylesheet defines, yours or the engine's")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V03`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `Unsafe.Html`, and whether what it puts in went through `sanitize`. Not a mistake — the one place markup enters, named so a review finds every one") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Structure", id: "structure").h3

        Markdown("Errors, and they have no codes because each is a reference to something that does not exist: an unknown component, prop, flag, case, part, event or slot; a fill for a slot the component does not declare; two positional arguments; a `layout:` naming a component with no default slot; two pages or two components with one name; an `emit` of an undeclared event. Each names what it takes instead.\n\nAn `env` name that has not been made public, read from a page, is also an error — see [chapter 19](/docs/guide/security#env-and-what-ends-up-in-the-bundle).")

        Heading("wf fmt", id: "wf-fmt").h2

        Markdown("Formats every `.wf` and `.wfx` under a path (or one file) to the canonical layout: four-space indents, one statement per line, clause order `style` → `transition` → `on` → fills → children, spaces after commas and around operators. `--check` fails without writing, for CI; `--stdout` prints one file. `--to wfx` rewrites a `.wf` project in the indented layout (and renames the files), `--to wf` the other way; both are lossless.")

        Heading("wf test", id: "wf-test").h2

        Markdown("A test declaration renders a body with the project's components, stores, types and constants at hand, and holds the result to what it expects:")

        CodeBlock(#"component PriceTag(_ amount: Number, sale: Bool = false) {
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Text(format(amount, .currency)).bold
        if sale { Badge("Sale").danger }
    }
}

test "a price tag shows the amount as currency" {
    PriceTag(1234.5)
    expect "$1,234.50"
    expect not "Sale"
}

test "a sale tag carries the badge" {
    PriceTag(10, sale: true)
    expect "Sale"
}

test "a list renders each row"(data: { rows: [{ name: "a" }, { name: "b" }] }) {
    for r in rows { Text(r.name) }
    expect "a"
    expect "b"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- Tests live in `tests/*.wf` (or beside the code in `src/`).\n- `expect \"text\"` must appear in what the page shows; `expect not \"text\"` must not.\n- `data: \{ … \}` supplies values by name; a `state` in the body is seeded with its initial value.\n- Each render is also compared to `tests/__snapshots__/<file>/<test>.html`, written when missing; `--update` rewrites them after an intended change.\n\n`wf test` exits non-zero on a failure, with a diff.")

        Heading("A test that acts", id: "a-test-that-acts").h3

        Markdown("Everything above only **looks**: the body is rendered and the expects read the result. A click cannot be rendered — it runs a handler, which runs an action, which changes a store, which repaints — so a test with an interaction in it is compiled into a real page, served, and run in a headless browser. Same compiler, same runtime, same output a reader gets.")

        CodeBlock(#"test "reporting an incident opens one more" {
    use IncidentStore
    state draft = ""
    Text("{IncidentStore.open} open")
    Input(bind: draft, label: "What happened")
    Button("Report") { on click { IncidentStore.report(draft) } }

    expect "2 open"
    type "Queue backing up" into "What happened"
    click "Report"
    expect "3 open"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Table(caption: "A test that acts") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Step").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`expect \"text\"` · `expect not \"text\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("What the page must, or must not, show")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`click \"Save\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Clicks whatever carries that name")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`type \"Ada\" into \"Name\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Types into the control that label names")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`press \"Enter\"` · `press \"Escape\" in \"Search\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("A key, on the focused element or a named one")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The steps run **in the order written** — that is the whole meaning, since what a click did is only visible in the expect that follows it.\n\nEverything is found the way a reader finds it: by the name on it. A button is its text; a control is its label, its placeholder or its `aria-label`. That is the same name the accessibility checks hold a page to, so a test that passes here is a page somebody can use — and a test that cannot find what it asks for says so:")

        CodeBlock(#"  FAIL  tests/cart.wf — the count follows what is added
        nothing to click called "Publish"
        the page showed:
        Nothing here"#, title: "")

        Markdown("**A handler that throws fails the test**, even where the expects would have passed — a click that quietly did nothing is the bug, not a pass. These tests need a Chrome or Chromium on the machine, or `WF_CHROME` pointing at one; a test that only looks needs neither.\n\nA test that acts takes no snapshot. What it expects is the test.")

        Heading("wf generate", id: "wf-generate").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf generate page Pricing")
            ])

        Markdown("writes `src/pages/Pricing.wf` with a page declaring `path: \"/pricing\"`, a title and an `h1`; `component` and `store` likewise. It is a starting point, not a framework — edit freely.")

        Heading("wf docs, wf registry, wf types", id: "wf-docs-wf-registry-wf-types").h2

        Markdown("`wf docs` writes `docs/index.html`: a gallery of every built-in with its props, cases, flags, events, slots and parts, and every component, enum, type, store and page the project declares, with their docs (`///` comments). `wf registry --json` and `wf types --json` print the same data for tools — a design tool, a generator, a script:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf registry --json | jq '.components[] | select(.name == \"Button\") | .props[].name'")
            ])

        Markdown("The registry is the compiler's own table: what it prints is exactly what the checker accepts.")

        Heading("Editors", id: "editors").h2

        Markdown("`wf-lsp` is the language server, built from `crates/wf-lsp` (`cargo install --path crates/wf-lsp`, or the binary from a release). It gives:\n\n- Diagnostics as you type — every check `wf build` runs.\n- Completion: components and their props after `(`, flags and parts after `.`, enum cases after `:`, events after `on `, `$` tokens, `match` arms, slot names, fields of a record, methods of a string or a list.\n- Hover: a component's doc and signature, a name's inferred type.\n- Go to definition, find references, rename across the project.\n- Document symbols and the outline.\n- Quick fixes — \"Change to `…`\" on a misspelt name — and `Extract component` on a selection.\n\n**Zed**: the extension is in `editors/zed`; install it with `zed: install dev extension` pointing at that folder, or from the extension registry. It brings Tree-sitter highlighting, outline, brackets, indentation, snippets and the language server (found on `PATH` or downloaded from the latest release), for `.wf` and `.wfx`.\n\n**VS Code**: `editors/vscode` — TextMate highlighting and the language server. Open the folder and run `Extensions: Install from VSIX` on the packaged extension, or symlink it into `~/.vscode/extensions`.\n\nAny other editor with LSP support runs `wf-lsp` over stdio.")

        Heading("Project layout, as a reminder", id: "project-layout-as-a-reminder").h2

        CodeBlock(#"webfluent.app.json    config
src/                  .wf / .wfx / .md pages, components, stores, themes, .css, translations/
public/               copied to the output root
tests/                wf test declarations and __snapshots__/
dist/                 the build (build.output)"#, title: "")
    }
}
